refhtml=''.join(f'<article class="research-card"><strong>{escape(n)}</strong><p>{escape(desc)}. Source: {escape(credit)}.</p><a href="{link}" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Study reference ↗</a></article>' for n,credit,link,desc in refs)
research=begin('BRUME / research & decisions','Documented creative directions and cited photographic campaign inspiration')+'''<section class="mast"><div class="wrap"><span class="cap">CREATIVE STRATEGY / A CHANGE OF LANGUAGE</span><h1>Another way<br><i>to see.</i></h1><div class="hero-grid"><img class="mast-photo" src="images/studio-contact-sheet.jpg" alt="Four-view generated contact sheet of sculptural lamp photography"><div class="mast-copy"><p>Why the rejected NODO project was not enough—and what changed in BRUME.</p><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:14px">The previous project relied on oversized sans headings, flat-color vector panels and repeatable icon compositions. BRUME makes imagery, lighting, texture, product consistency and editing rhythm the main design decisions.</p></div></div></div></section><section class="section"><div class="wrap"><div class="chapter"><span class="eyebrow">01 / VISUAL DIFFERENTIATION</span><div><h2>More than<br><em>a new palette.</em></h2><p class="lead">A photographic story, rather than another flat-graphics identity.</p><p>AFTERFORM already has substantial fashion-oriented brand and print work. INTERVAL explores bold cultural typographic campaign systems. PROOF uses operational blue/citron status graphics. Áurea leans editorial and fragrance-led. NODO repeated the oversized typographic/colored vector formula. BRUME instead centers atmosphere and material with a three-edition glass silhouette.</p><p>These are creative assessments grounded in accessible portfolio source and preview content, not formal user research or a comprehensive screenshot-by-screenshot critique.</p></div></div><div class="grid3"><article class="story-panel"><h3>A. Photographic atmosphere</h3><p><strong>Selected.</strong> Ambient light, glass texture, room context, cinematic shadow and narrow editorial type.</p></article><article class="story-panel"><h3>B. Mixed-media paper</h3><p>Photocopy collage, hand cut textures, torn folds and tactile marks. Distinct, but less clear product consistency for a lighting range.</p></article><article class="story-panel"><h3>C. Material laboratory</h3><p>Abstract glass studies, optical refractions, specimen grids and manufacturing references. Striking but less emotionally communicative.</p></article></div></div></section><section class="section alt"><div class="wrap"><div class="chapter"><span class="eyebrow">02 / REFERENCES STUDIED</span><div><h2>Look outward.<br><em>Make it new.</em></h2><p class="lead">Reference work is used to interpret process, not to recreate anyone's campaign.</p><p>Pinterest references were used as discovery starting points; studio case studies and first-party work descriptions provided more reliable information about how the photography and packaging were art-directed. Only sources actually researched are listed here. Images and logos from these sources are not republished or represented as our own.</p></div></div><div class="grid2">'''+refhtml+'''</div></div></section><section class="section"><div class="wrap"><div class="chapter"><span class="eyebrow">03 / HONEST PROVENANCE</span><div><h2>Proof, and<br><em>its limits.</em></h2><p>Four photo scenes were generated with an image-generation model: a PLUM room, IVORY daylight, a three-edition study and an AMBER detail. Their visual consistency serves an illustrative art-direction concept. There is no built lamp, branded industrial design validation, actual studio photography, manufacturing certification or tested audience.</p><p>All campaign vectors and email content are separately authored, not generated as text inside images. Technical print output is exploratory and cannot substitute for a printer-certified job.</p></div></div></div></section>'''+footer
(root/'research.html').write_text(research)
# Save site-oriented production info
(root/'README.md').write_text('''# BRUME — Photography-led portfolio replacement\n\nThis is a complete self-directed, AI-assisted design concept for a fictional sculptural lighting studio, replacing the rejected NODO art direction.\n\n- `index.html` — photographic campaign case study, 15 live-image SVG artwork masters and three HTML emails.\n- `production.html` — 3 edition labels, 4 print collateral pieces, simulated archived revisions and technical print caveats.\n- `research.html` — portfolio differentiation, three moodboard approaches and cited original creative references.\n- `social/*.svg` — editable compositions with linked AI-generated photographic assets; `social/*.png` are flattened counterparts.\n- `images/*.jpg` — four conceptual image-generated product scenes. Images do **not** document manufactured lighting products or a real photo shoot.\n- `email/*.html` & `email/*.txt` — three standalone table-based mock emails, with sample links. Clients and legal footer not validated for a real send.\n- `print/svg/` & `print/pdf/` — dimensioned source graphics and specimen vector PDFs; print-specific CMYK spot decisions, output profiles, packaging dies, and electrical safety not validated.\n- `build_assets.py`, `build_email_print.py`, `build_site.py` — reproducible authoring scripts. These use system-installed fonts; font files are not included.\n\n## Visual change\n\nNODO = flat vectors / loud display sans / color blocks / product-icons. BRUME = photographed glass / warm cinematic atmosphere / small editorial serif / material and context. The campaign emphasizes different photographic crops and rhythms rather than repeating one layout.\n\n## Scope and verification\n\nSource assets, relative paths, responsive rules and PDF geometry are programmatically inspectable. Full professional accessibility, actual email-client QA, real printer preflight, physical production and user testing have not been completed.\n\nTo preview: run `python -m http.server` from this directory and open `index.html`. GitHub Pages can serve the files as a static `/brume/` subdirectory.\n''')
